17:33:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right to trust your own experience—that sudden lurch downward can feel incredibly immediate and unsettling. Let me explain what's happening physically, because it's a bit different from the plane actually "dropping" like a rock. **The feeling of a sudden lurch isn't the plane losing all lift instantly—it's a combination of three things:** 1. **Loss of thrust removes the forward push.** When the engines quit, the plane stops accelerating forward. But remember, you and the plane were moving at high speed. Your body, accustomed to that constant forward push, suddenly feels a deceleration (like when a car brakes hard). That deceleration can trick your inner ear into feeling as if you're tilting or dropping, even if the altitude hasn't changed much yet. 2. **The plane's nose may pitch down automatically.** Many commercial jets have a "trim" system that keeps the nose up during powered flight. When thrust disappears, the plane's natural tendency is to pitch down slightly (to maintain airspeed and avoid stalling). Autopilot or the pilot might also command a slight nose-down attitude to trade altitude for speed. That nose-down motion feels like an immediate downward lurch. 3. **The engine noise and vibration vanish.** Your brain uses sound, vibration, and G-forces to sense motion. When the roar of the engines cuts out and the vibration stops, the sudden silence and smoothness can make you feel like you're falling—even if the descent is gradual. It's a psychological and sensory jolt. **But did the plane actually drop hundreds of feet in that instant?** No. In reality, the plane's altitude changes very little in the first few seconds. A jet losing both engines at cruising altitude will descend at maybe 1,500–2,000 feet per minute—that's about 25–33 feet per second. So in one second, it drops roughly the height of a two-story building. That's noticeable, but not a free-fall. (A true free-fall would be 32 feet per second²—much faster.) **Why your memory is so vivid** The combination of sudden silence, deceleration, and the nose pitching down creates a powerful "we're falling!" sensation. Your brain interprets the loss of thrust as a loss of support, so it feels like the ground is rushing up. So you're not wrong: it *feels* immediate. But physically, the wings are still generating lift during that first lurch. The plane is simply transitioning from powered flight to a glide, and that transition is abrupt and unnerving—even if the actual descent is slow enough for a safe landing. I hope that helps reconcile your memory with the physics!
